Test non-collinear Fe in LCAO basis with multiple k points, moments along +-y.

This is scf_angle_spin4 with the initial moments globally rotated by 90 degrees
about z (+-x -> +-y). Without spin-orbit coupling the spin frame is decoupled
from the lattice, so every reference value must equal the scf_angle_spin4 one;
result.ref is that file, unchanged.

## Why the +-y twin is needed

m_x and m_z are read off the real part of the spinor density-matrix block and
are invariant under the conjugation convention of that block. Only m_y reads
the imaginary part. The +-x case therefore cannot detect a sign error in the
sigma_y loop between the Hamiltonian assembly (clx_j in merge_hr_part_to_hR)
and the density extraction (func_xyz_to_updown), which is how that convention
changed three times -- #7513, #7664, #7832 -- without any test reacting. See
issues #7759 and #7831.

Reintroducing the #7664 m_y sign on top of develop 3937023d6 moves this case
by 3.19 eV (-6264.2727 vs -6267.4652), drops AMAG from 6.70362 to 3.9e-05 and
stops it converging, while scf_angle_spin4 does not move at all.

## Why ks_solver is pinned to scalapack_gvx

With scalapack_gvx this case is bitwise identical to scf_angle_spin4 at both
np=2 and np=4 (-6267.4651888505, AMAG 6.70362, 12 iterations), which is the
exact rotational invariance the case is meant to assert.

With genelpa -- the default when ELPA is available, and what scf_angle_spin4
itself uses -- the +-y case does NOT converge (hits scf_nmax=50) and settles
BELOW the true ground state by an np-dependent amount:

  solver          np   E_KohnSham (eV)     AMAG      iters
  scalapack_gvx   2    -6267.4651888505    6.70362   12
  scalapack_gvx   4    -6267.4651888505    6.70362   12
  genelpa         2    -6269.6774946269    6.80171   50 (no convergence)
  genelpa         4    -6267.7588409455    6.76183   50 (no convergence)

A variational method cannot go below the ground state, and the result must not
depend on the rank count, so this is a solver defect, not a physics result. It
is the genelpa complex generalized transform reading the stale lower triangle
of a Hermitian matrix for which LCAO only fills the upper triangle -- defect 2
of #7913. The +-x case is insensitive to it because H_{up,down} = B_x is real
there, whereas for +-y it is purely imaginary (-i*B_y).

Remove this pin once #7913 is fixed, so that the case also guards the genelpa
path.
